TAM-CLIF -- TERMINAL E-MAIL CLIENT

tam-clif is a terminal e-mail client (MUA) written in C17, depending only
on the standard C libraries (POSIX libc) and libtam
(https://github.com/greenermoose/libtam). Inspired by aerc
(https://git.sr.ht/~rjarry/aerc), tam-clif brings asynchronous,
keyboard-driven, multi-account email reading and composition to Tamlinux
with sub-megabyte memory overhead and first-class Linux virtual console
(TERM=linux) support.

Status: Planning & initial repository setup (pre-v0.1.0). No C source files
have been written yet; see ROADMAP.md for the phased design and
implementation plan.

Follows the tam-idol 1.3.0 standard
(https://github.com/greenermoose/tam-idol).


DESIGN PHILOSOPHY

- Zero Bloat, Pure C17 + libtam:
  Links statically against libtam.a (tam_str, tam_fs, tam_path, tam_exec,
  tam_ini, tam_tty, tam_key, tam_uni). No Go runtime, no Python, no
  ncurses, no GLib, and no OpenSSL build dependency in the core binary.

- Compose with Unix Mail Tools:
  Rather than embedding a full TLS/IMAP/SMTP stack in the TUI binary,
  tam-clif reads local Maildir/Maildir++ stores directly and delegates
  network sync, sending, OAuth2, and MIME filtering via tam_exec to
  focused system tools (such as mbsync/isync, msmtp, sendmail, tam-ring,
  gpg, and w3m).

- Asynchronous UI:
  Inspired by aerc's worker model, slow I/O (directory scans, sync
  subprocesses, MIME filter pipelines, and mail sending) runs
  asynchronously off the UI loop so navigation never freezes.

- First-Class Linux VT & Modern Terminal Support:
  Built on tam_tty, tam_key, and tam_uni for clean rendering on bare
  Linux VTs (TERM=linux, 8/16 colors) as well as modern terminal emulators
  (foot, synchronized frames, 256/truecolor).


KEY IDEAS ADOPTED FROM AERC

- Tabbed Multi-Account Workspace:
  Isolated per-account configuration in accounts.ini with independent tabs
  for personal, work, and mailing-list contexts.

- Vim-Like Modal Ergonomics & Ex-Command Line:
  Context-aware bindings ([default], [messages], [view], [compose])
  configured via binds.ini, paired with an interactive : command bar
  (:filter, :query, :pipe, :save, :compose, :cf, :quit).

- External Filter Pipeline:
  Match MIME types or headers in [filters] (e.g., text/html, text/patch,
  application/pgp-signature) and pipe parts through external formatters
  before display.

- Client-Side Threading:
  Fast References / In-Reply-To / Subject threading over local Maildir
  indexes with collapsible threads.

- Template-Driven Composition & $EDITOR:
  Generate reply/forward headers and quoted bodies from templates, edit in
  $EDITOR (such as tam-edit or vi), and review/attach parts before sending.

- First-Class Patch & Mailing-List Workflows:
  Pipe patches directly to git am or git apply, colorize diffs, and
  navigate threads cleanly.


PLANNED CONFIGURATION & PATHS

Configuration and state follow XDG conventions via tam_xdg_path:

  $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/tamlinux/tam-clif/tam-clif.ini   UI, threading, [filters]
  $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/tamlinux/tam-clif/accounts.ini   Account definitions
  $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/tamlinux/tam-clif/binds.ini      Context keybindings


BUILD PREREQUISITES (PLANNED)

- C17 compiler (gcc or clang)
- POSIX libc (glibc or musl) and GNU Make
- libtam (libtam.a)

See ROADMAP.md for the milestone breakdown from v0.1.0 to v1.0.0.


